  "Number": 5,
  "Question": "For real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b,\\) define \\(a\\,\\$\\,b=(a-b)^2.\\) What is \\((x-y)^2\\,\\$\\,(y-x)^2?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "\\(x^2+y^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(2x^2\\)",
    "D": "\\(2y^2\\)",
    "E": "\\(4xy\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The two inputs to the operation are equal. Indeed, \\(y-x=-(x-y)\\), and a number and its opposite have the same square, so\n\\[\n(y-x)^2=(x-y)^2.\n\\]\nThe rule \\(a\\,\\$\\,b=(a-b)^2\\) therefore gives\n\\[\n(x-y)^2\\,\\$\\,(y-x)^2\n=\\bigl((x-y)^2-(y-x)^2\\bigr)^2\n=0.\n\\]\nThis holds for every real \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
